// Additional languages, kept in their own module so translations.ts stays
// readable — it already holds the original five (en/es/fr/zh/te) plus the
// full key union, and merging fifteen more inline would make that file
// unmanageable. TRANSLATIONS in translations.ts spreads this in.
//
// IMPORTANT — READ BEFORE RELYING ON THESE:
//
// These translations are machine-generated by an AI (me), not produced or
// reviewed by native or fluent speakers of any of these languages. They
// cover the app's HIGH-TRAFFIC keys — navigation, buttons, the Dashboard,
// auth, and common actions — not the full key set. Anything not translated
// here falls back to English automatically (see LanguageContext's t()),
// which is a deliberate, safe default: a missing key shows readable English
// rather than a blank or a raw key name.
//
// Treat the widely-spoken European languages here as "probably close" and
// everything else as "good enough to ship and iterate on." Before marketing
// the app in any of these locales, have a fluent speaker review the column
// — machine translation reliably gets tone, formality register, and
// fitness-domain terms subtly wrong in ways that read as unprofessional to
// a native speaker even when the literal meaning is correct.

